-  "research_note": "CURRENT T-3055 ROOF/ATTIC REFINEMENT: a reconstructed25.2-degree working pitch lowers the Sauganash roof toward the Andreas drawing under the retained provisional camera. The pictured attic sash is lower and slightly wider; its new numeric choices stay reconstructed. Main footprint, wall height, geographic placement, phase dates, source-dependent colours/shutters and whole-picture grade remain unchanged. The original picture comparison still differs, and the drawing/camera/date limits remain unresolved.\n\nVERIFIED 1902 PICTURE SOURCE: trowbridge_sauganash_hotel is W. E. S. Trowbridge’s \"Mark Beaubien's Tavern, the Sauganash, in 1835\", independently verified in Edwin O. Gale’s 1902 book, facing printed p. 31. The artwork’s earlier rights hold is superseded by the actual title/copyright publication evidence. Its 1835 caption is retrospective and does not date every feature to 1 July 1835. Bright-blue shutters remain grounded in Wau-Bun’s separate recollection of 1831; louvred leaf detail remains inferred from this later drawing. The monochrome Andreas plate cannot independently establish colour. Original picture comparisons remain open; no dimensions, placement, geometry or confidence values change with this source verification.\n\nPRIOR RESEARCH RECORD:\nMILESTONE 0 REFERENCE RECORD. Two things are documented and vivid — the white two-story frame and the bright-blue shutters, both from Wau-Bun — and two things are unknown: the building's dimensions and whether it had a gallery. That combination is exactly why the per-attribute confidence model exists, and this record is deliberately the first one in the dataset. See docs/RESEARCH/sauganash_hotel.md for the source conflicts and the reasoning behind each reading. The Wigwam of 1860 stood on this site later; the hotel burned 4 March 1851.",
+  "research_note": "CURRENT T-3055 ROOF/ATTIC REFINEMENT: a reconstructed25.2-degree working pitch lowers the Sauganash roof toward the Andreas drawing under the retained provisional camera. The pictured attic sash is lower and slightly wider; its new numeric choices stay reconstructed. Main footprint, wall height, geographic placement, phase dates, source-dependent colours/shutters and whole-picture grade remain unchanged. The original picture comparison still differs, and the drawing/camera/date limits remain unresolved.\n\nVERIFIED 1902 PICTURE SOURCE: trowbridge_sauganash_hotel is W. E. S. Trowbridge’s \"Mark Beaubien's Tavern, the Sauganash, in 1835\", independently verified in Edwin O. Gale’s 1902 book, facing printed p. 31. The artwork’s earlier rights hold is superseded by the actual title/copyright publication evidence. Its 1835 caption is retrospective and does not date every feature to 1 July 1835. Bright-blue shutters remain grounded in Wau-Bun’s separate recollection of 1831; louvred leaf detail remains inferred from this later drawing. The monochrome Andreas plate cannot independently establish colour. Original picture comparisons remain open; no dimensions, placement, geometry or confidence values change with this source verification.\n\nPRIOR RESEARCH RECORD:\nMILESTONE 0 REFERENCE RECORD. Two things are documented and vivid — the white two-story frame and the bright-blue shutters, both from Wau-Bun — and two things are unknown: the building's dimensions and whether it had a gallery. That combination is exactly why the per-attribute confidence model exists, and this record is deliberately the first one in the dataset. See docs/RESEARCH/sauganash_hotel.md for the source conflicts and the reasoning behind each reading. The Wigwam of 1860 stood on this site later; the hotel burned 4 March 1851.\n\nAndreas's 1884 engraving shows the hotel behind the separate log cabin and is now held on this hotel's Images tab. The main and side-range roof shapes and the left gable details remain under review against this retrospective picture. It does not establish every feature's exact condition on 1 July 1835, a historical viewing position, or literal paint colours.",
